└─[1m[32m[34m$[0m[34m[39m [K[?1h=[?2004h# 1. Four core responsibilities of an OS:
# - Process management (scheduling programs on the CPU and keeping them isolated)[K
# - Memory management (allocating RAM safely and providing virtual memory)[K
# - File management (organizing storage into files/folders and enforcing permissions)[K
# - Device management (talking to hardware peripherals via uniform drivers)[K
[K
# 2. Why can't apps talk to hardware directly on a modern OS?[K
# Two reasons: safety and portability. If any app could touch hardware directly, a buggy or[K
# malicious program could wipe disks, read other apps' RAM, or crash the whole system.[K
# Also, apps would break across different hardware unless they bundled drivers for every device.[K
[K
# 3. What distinguishes a Real-Time OS (RTOS) from a general-purpose OS?[K
# A general-purpose OS (like Linux) focuses on fairness and smooth overall throughput across many tasks. [K
# An RTOS focuses on strict deadlines and predictability—a task must finish on time every single time,[K
# because a late response (like in a car airbag or motor controller) counts as a system failure.[K
[K
# 4. In -rwxr-xr--, what shows file type vs. permissions?[K
# - File type: Only the very first character ('-'), meaning it's a regular file (d = folder, l = link).[K
# - Permissions: The remaining 9 characters in three groups:[K
#   'rwx' = owner (read, write, execute), 'r-x' = group (read, execute), 'r--' = others (read only).[K
[K
# 5. What is /proc, and how is it different from /home?[K
