# 1. Create lab1 files (already created: a.txt, b.txt, c.txt)[K
touch a.txt b.txt c.txt[K
[K
# 2. Set permissions on a.txt (owner: rw, group: r, others: none)[K
chmod 640 a.txt[K
ls -l a.txt[K
# Why 640? read=4, write=2, execute=1 -> Owner=4+2=6 (rw-), Group=4 (r--), Others=0 (---).[K
[K
# 3. Inspect running kernel info[K
cat /proc/version[K
# Output shows 64-bit Linux kernel 7.2.15+kali-amd64, built with GCC 16.2.0,[K
# supporting multi-core (SMP) and dynamic preemption (Nov 3, 2026).[K
[K
# 4. Resource Manager vs. Extended Machine[K
# - Resource Manager: Referee sharing limited hardware (CPU, RAM, disk) safely among programs.[K
#   Lab example: chmod 640 and chown controlling access to a.txt.[K
# - Extended Machine: Hides messy hardware details behind clean interfaces (open, read, write).[K
#   Lab example: touch or cat /proc/version instead of reading raw disk sectors/RAM addresses.[K
[K
# 5. Why /dev/sda representing a physical disk as a file is useful[K
# Lets programs read/write raw storage using normal file calls (open, read, write)[K
# and standard tools (dd, cat) while reusing file permissions for security.[K
# Without it, every app would need custom drivers for every SATA/NVMe/USB controller[K
# and manage disk sectors, DMA, and hardware interrupts directly.[K
[K
# ==========================================[K
# Review Questions[K
# ==========================================[K
# 1. Four core OS responsibilities: Process management, Memory management,[K
#    File management, and Device management.[K
# 2. Apps cannot talk to hardware directly for safety/isolation (preventing crashes or[K
#    disk/RAM corruption) and portability across different hardware devices.[K
# 3. General-purpose OS optimizes for fairness/throughput; Real-Time OS (RTOS) optimizes[K
#    for strict deadlines and deterministic response times (late = failure).[K
# 4. In -rwxr-xr--: '-' is file type (regular file); 'rwx' = owner, 'r-x' = group, 'r--' = others.[K
# 5. /home stores persistent files on disk; /proc is a virtual in-memory filesystem (0 bytes on disk)[K
